# APM dependencies
apm_modules/
stacks/unifi-network/tailscale-acl.json
/headlamp/
# Runtime caches that must never be committed. xcproxy writes a ~20MB
# tmdb_cache.db into its resources/ dir, which has been committed by accident
# before. Bare pattern, so it matches at any depth.
*.db*
# Crew: ignore runtime state (logs, inbox, sessions)
# Note: .crew/casting/* is identity and MUST be committed to main, not ignored
.crew/orchestration-log/
.crew/log/
.crew/decisions/inbox/
.crew/sessions/
.crew/.scratch/
.crew/.cache/
# Crew: state owned by crew-state branch (two-layer/orphan backend)
.crew/decisions.md
.crew/agents/*/history.md
# /Crew: state owned by crew-state branch
# Crew: SubCrew activation file (local to this machine)
.crew-workstream

# Pkl VS Code extension VSIX cache (installed via the mise `pkl-vscode` task)
.pkl-vscode/

# Talos / Kubernetes client credentials.
#
# `talosconfig` carries an unencrypted `os:admin` client certificate and its
# private key — full machine-API control: config read/write, reboot, reset,
# and machine secrets. It must never be committed. talos/clusterconfig/.gitignore
# scopes its rule to that subdirectory only, which once left talos/talosconfig
# one level up uncovered — and it got committed. Bare patterns here, so they
# match at any depth.
kubeconfig
talosconfig

# Plaintext SOPS scratch files. The VS Code SOPS extension writes a decrypted
# twin next to the encrypted file as `.decrypted~<name>.sops.yaml` while you
# edit it, and leaves it behind if the editor dies. Bare pattern, so it matches
# at any depth; the trailing glob covers .sops.json/.sops.env twins too.
.decrypted~*

# graphify build artifacts that must not be shared between machines.
#
# `.graphify_python` and `.graphify_root` are written by the skill with absolute
# paths — the interpreter under ~/.cache/uv and the scan root, which on a
# worktree run is the worktree, not the repo. Committed, they point every other
# clone at a path that does not exist there. The skill rewrites both on each run,
# so losing them costs nothing.
#
# `cache/` is the AST extraction cache, keyed by content hash. It is derived
# entirely from tracked source and rebuilds in seconds, but a full run churns
# thousands of files and several MB into a public repo for no benefit.
graphify-out/.graphify_python
graphify-out/.graphify_root
graphify-out/cache/

# Dated graph snapshots (`graphify-out/YYYY-MM-DD/`).
#
# These broke every Pulumi stack in the estate on 2026-09-05. Each is a ~24 MiB
# copy of graph.json, they accrete about one a week, and nobody adds one on
# purpose -- they ride along in whatever PR happens to run the sweep, which is
# why 2026-08-28 arrived in the agentboard PR and 2026-09-05 in a coder fix.
#
# The Pulumi Kubernetes operator's workspace init fetches the repo as a Flux
# source artifact and refuses anything over 100 MiB:
#
#   unable to fetch flux artifact: failed to extract archive
#   tar "graphify-out/graph.json" is bigger than max archive size of 104857600 bytes
#
# The third snapshot took the repo from 95.7 MiB to 119.7 MiB and every
# workspace pod went Init:Error -- all nine stacks stopped reconciling, silently,
# for hours, since a stack that never runs looks identical to one with nothing
# to do.
#
# The snapshots are pure derived data: `graphify update .` rebuilds the current
# graph from tracked source, and no file in this repo references a dated path.
# So they are ignored rather than retained -- keeping N of them would just move
# the cliff, at 24 MiB a step.
graphify-out/[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]/
